WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.909
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.209 --> 00:00:08.433
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:17.039
Unit fourteen: Freedom and Its Limits. In this unit, you will examine the philosophical limits of individual freedom.

4
00:00:18.050 --> 00:00:19.411
negative liberty.

5
00:00:20.111 --> 00:00:23.666
negative liberty means freedom from interference by others.

6
00:00:23.966 --> 00:00:27.034
Negative liberty protects us from state control.

7
00:00:27.734 --> 00:00:30.758
Your turn. Say: negative liberty.

8
00:00:33.458 --> 00:00:34.807
negative liberty.

9
00:00:35.817 --> 00:00:37.189
positive liberty.

10
00:00:37.889 --> 00:00:42.443
positive liberty means the freedom and ability to achieve one's goals.

11
00:00:42.743 --> 00:00:45.335
Education expands positive liberty.

12
00:00:46.035 --> 00:00:49.685
Your turn. Say: positive liberty.

13
00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:53.677
positive liberty.

14
00:00:54.683 --> 00:00:55.951
harm principle.

15
00:00:56.651 --> 00:01:02.831
harm principle means Mill's idea that freedom may be limited only to prevent harm to others.

16
00:01:03.131 --> 00:01:06.732
The harm principle justifies seatbelt laws only partly.

17
00:01:07.432 --> 00:01:10.479
Your turn. Say: harm principle.

18
00:01:13.179 --> 00:01:14.331
harm principle.

19
00:01:15.317 --> 00:01:16.353
paternalism.

20
00:01:17.053 --> 00:01:21.363
paternalism means limiting people's freedom supposedly for their own good.

21
00:01:21.663 --> 00:01:25.311
Banning sugary drinks is accused of paternalism.

22
00:01:26.011 --> 00:01:29.185
Your turn. Say: paternalism.

23
00:01:31.885 --> 00:01:32.910
paternalism.

24
00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:35.382
civil disobedience.

25
00:01:36.082 --> 00:01:42.076
civil disobedience means peacefully breaking unjust laws as a protest.

26
00:01:42.376 --> 00:01:46.128
Gandhi's salt march was an act of civil disobedience.

27
00:01:46.828 --> 00:01:50.374
Your turn. Say: civil disobedience.

28
00:01:53.074 --> 00:01:54.668
civil disobedience.

29
00:01:55.683 --> 00:01:57.265
conscientious objection.

30
00:01:57.965 --> 00:02:02.670
conscientious objection means refusing to take part in something for moral reasons.

31
00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:07.941
Conscientious objection to military service is recognised in some countries.

32
00:02:08.641 --> 00:02:12.455
Your turn. Say: conscientious objection.

33
00:02:15.155 --> 00:02:16.806
conscientious objection.

34
00:02:17.817 --> 00:02:19.212
libertarianism.

35
00:02:19.912 --> 00:02:26.103
libertarianism means a philosophy prioritising individual freedom and minimal government.

36
00:02:26.403 --> 00:02:29.424
Libertarianism opposes most taxation.

37
00:02:30.124 --> 00:02:33.706
Your turn. Say: libertarianism.

38
00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:37.627
libertarianism.

39
00:02:38.617 --> 00:02:40.117
social liberalism.

40
00:02:40.817 --> 00:02:47.855
social liberalism means a view combining individual freedom with social support.

41
00:02:48.155 --> 00:02:51.223
Social liberalism supports public healthcare.

42
00:02:51.923 --> 00:02:55.295
Your turn. Say: social liberalism.

43
00:02:57.995 --> 00:02:59.495
social liberalism.

44
00:03:00.483 --> 00:03:02.669
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:02.969 --> 00:03:06.733
Ms. Sarah, should people be free to do anything that doesn't hurt others?

46
00:03:08.017 --> 00:03:13.397
That's Mill's harm principle. Attractive though it is, it raises hard questions.

47
00:03:14.683 --> 00:03:15.533
Such as?

48
00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:23.879
Whether seatbelt laws are justified. They protect the driver, admittedly, yet they limit freedom.

49
00:03:25.183 --> 00:03:26.707
Isn't that paternalism?

50
00:03:28.017 --> 00:03:33.354
It is. Even so, most people accept it. Libertarians don't, of course.

51
00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.591
And breaking unjust laws?

52
00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:43.621
Civil disobedience can be justified, albeit at a personal cost to those who practise it.

53
00:03:44.917 --> 00:03:48.031
Grammar focus: Concession and contrast.

54
00:03:48.331 --> 00:03:51.445
Sophisticated concession shows balanced thinking.

55
00:03:51.745 --> 00:04:00.446
Attractive though it is, the principle raises hard questions. It can be justified, albeit at a personal cost.

56
00:04:00.746 --> 00:04:04.754
Admittedly, yet and even so also signal concession.

57
00:04:05.054 --> 00:04:10.455
Fronted concessive clauses ('Attractive though it is') are a hallmark of polished C2 argument.

58
00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:14.218
Now read and listen. How Free Should We Be?.

59
00:04:14.518 --> 00:04:19.258
Philosopher Isaiah Berlin distinguished two concepts of liberty.

60
00:04:20.550 --> 00:04:27.623
Negative liberty means freedom from interference; positive liberty means the capacity to pursue one's goals.

61
00:04:28.917 --> 00:04:36.083
John Stuart Mill's harm principle holds that society may restrict freedom only to prevent harm to others.

62
00:04:37.383 --> 00:04:41.914
Simple though it sounds, the principle is difficult to apply in practice.

63
00:04:43.217 --> 00:04:50.963
Laws on seatbelts or smoking are often criticised as paternalism, albeit popular paternalism.

64
00:04:52.250 --> 00:05:00.995
Libertarianism emphasises negative liberty, while social liberalism seeks to expand positive liberty through public services.

65
00:05:02.283 --> 00:05:08.486
Civil disobedience and conscientious objection test the limits of a citizen's duty to obey.

66
00:05:09.783 --> 00:05:16.729
Freedom, admittedly, is cherished everywhere; yet its boundaries remain endlessly contested.

67
00:05:18.017 --> 00:05:20.495
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:20.795 --> 00:05:22.794
Freedom from interference is blank.

69
00:05:23.094 --> 00:05:28.176
A, negative liberty. B, positive liberty. C, paternalism.

70
00:05:32.183 --> 00:05:34.589
The answer is A: negative liberty.

71
00:05:36.083 --> 00:05:38.814
blank it may seem, the idea is complex.

72
00:05:39.114 --> 00:05:45.020
A, Simple as. B, As simple. C, Simple so.

73
00:05:49.017 --> 00:05:51.643
The answer is A: Simple as.

74
00:05:53.150 --> 00:05:54.824
The plan worked, blank slowly.

75
00:05:55.124 --> 00:05:59.892
A, albeit. B, although it. C, despite.

76
00:06:03.883 --> 00:06:06.045
The answer is A: albeit.

77
00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:10.920
Peacefully breaking unjust laws as protest is blank.

78
00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:16.974
A, civil disobedience. B, libertarianism. C, harm principle.

79
00:06:20.983 --> 00:06:24.248
The answer is A: civil disobedience.

80
00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:29.932
According to the text, who distinguished two concepts of liberty?

81
00:06:30.232 --> 00:06:33.897
A, Mill. B, Isaiah Berlin. C, Gandhi.

82
00:06:37.883 --> 00:06:40.498
The answer is B: Isaiah Berlin.

83
00:06:41.983 --> 00:06:47.933
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should governments limit freedom to protect people from themselves?

84
00:06:53.233 --> 00:06:58.019
Part two: Use two concessive structures to present a balanced view.

85
00:07:04.319 --> 00:07:20.047
Paternalistic though it may be, some regulation is justified, for example on seatbelts. Admittedly, it restricts choice; even so, the social cost of injuries affects everyone. Such laws should remain limited, albeit not abolished.

86
00:07:20.347 --> 00:07:22.323
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:23.317 --> 00:07:28.117
Great work today! You learned eight new words and concession and contrast.

88
00:07:28.417 --> 00:07:32.091
Today's value is respect. Thank the people who help us.

89
00:07:32.391 --> 00:07:35.531
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:35.831 --> 00:07:39.772
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

